7. Training data vs testing data
Why don't we evaluate a model only on the same data it learned from?
Because the model may memorize the training examples instead of learning patterns that generalize to new examples.
Training data	Testing data
Used to fit the model	Used for final evaluation
Model learns from it	Kept separate during training
Measures learning performance	Estimates performance on unseen data
A validation set is also often used to compare models and tune hyperparameters before final testing.
A common split is 80% training and 20% testing, but the best split depends on dataset size, task, and evaluation requirements.
8. What are overfitting and underfitting?
These are two common problems in Machine Learning.
Overfitting
The model learns the training data too closely, including noise or accidental patterns, and performs poorly on new data.
Example: A student memorizes the exact answers to practice questions but cannot solve a new question.
Common remedies include regularization, more representative training data, and cross-validation.

Underfitting
The model is too simple or insufficiently trained to capture important patterns.
Example: A student studies only one basic formula and cannot solve most exam questions.
Possible remedies include a more suitable model, better features, or improved training.


